      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town grew around the Great Northern Railway, which extended lines through Lakota between 1904 and 1915, linking it to markets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lakota_north_dakota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During and after World War I, Lakota's wheat prices hit $1.20–$1.50 per bushel, spurred by mechanization like steam threshing and early tractors by 1925.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lakota_north_dakota",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Lakota Farmers' Elevator, founded between 1910 and 1917, became a key cooperative grain storage hub for local farmers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lakota_north_dakota",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Lakota's local press began with the Lakota Herald in 1899, expanding in the 1920s, and later joined by the Lakota Journal and Optimist.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/lakota_north_dakota",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Lakota's population rose from 907 in 1940 to a peak of 1,066 in 1960, helped by the GI Bill and farm mechanization.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
